## 12. LIGHTING AND MATERIAL QUALITY

The scene must feel physically convincing and cinematographically lit.

Use a warm key light positioned above and slightly to one side of the typewriter.

Add soft fill illumination so the dark enamel and black metal parts remain visible.

Use a subtle rim light to separate the machinery and the diary from the background.

Create realistic contact shadows beneath the machine, on the desk, under the paper, and between the pages of the diary.

Use controlled metallic reflections on brass components. Their highlights should move naturally as the camera or objects move.

The leather should have tactile microtexture and soft roughness variation. The paper should diffuse warm light, revealing its fibres without making the text hard to read.

Avoid heavy bloom, excessive glow, overly reflective metal, plastic-looking leather, and shadows so dark that mechanical details disappear.

During writing, prioritize clear text rendering over cinematic depth of field.

## 13. CAMERA SYSTEM AND TRANSITIONS

Create four deliberate camera compositions.

**LIBRARY INTRO:** A low, cinematic three-quarter view of the writing desk, typewriter, and diary.

**TYPEWRITER HERO:** A close three-quarter view emphasizing the mechanical keyboard, paper, carriage, and materials.

**ACTIVE WRITING:** A stable close view that clearly shows the typing keys, actual text appearing on the paper, and the paper-feed mechanism.

**DIARY ARCHIVE:** An overhead three-quarter view of the open antique diary and its preserved pages.

Transition between the camera compositions using smoothly eased movement.

During ordinary writing, keep the camera mostly stable. Avoid automatic orbiting, constant zooming, or camera movements that make typing uncomfortable.

During the paper-archiving animation, guide the camera subtly from the machine toward the diary, ensuring the transfer remains visible.

Return to the active-writing composition only after the animation has completed and the application is ready for further input.

## 14. UI/UX — MODERN FUNCTIONALITY WITH AN ANTIQUE SOUL

Design a refined interface that complements the scene instead of competing with it.

Use small, legible, well-spaced interface elements with understated borders and restrained brass accents.

The interface may contain:

- The “THE MEMORY ATELIER” wordmark.
- A discreet current-entry title.
- The current date.
- A writing-status indicator.
- A word and character count.
- An archive access control.
- A new-sheet or new-entry action.
- A save or archive action.
- A subtle audio toggle.
- A reduced-motion or animation-skip setting.

The interface must never obscure essential text or cover the typewriter's mechanisms.

During active writing, the actual paper and the mechanical response should dominate the view.

Use unobtrusive visual feedback to communicate when an entry is being edited, when saving is in progress, and when the save has successfully completed.

Do not show a successful-saving state until the actual save operation has returned success.

All primary actions should work with mouse, keyboard, and touch where appropriate. Do not rely exclusively on hover to communicate functionality.

## 15. ACTUAL FUNCTIONALITY AND DATA HANDLING

Treat the 3D scene and the journaling application as two connected but separate systems.

The 3D scene is responsible for the physical typewriter, the mechanical animation, the paper, the diary, and the visual transitions.

The application layer is responsible for capturing real keyboard input, maintaining the text, formatting pages, saving entries, retrieving old entries, and tracking which pages belong to each entry.

Implement the writing interface with a genuine editable input rather than placing the user's entire text into a static 3D texture.

Keep the entry text in application state so that it can be edited, saved, retrieved, and rendered correctly.

For every completed page, maintain an association between:
- The actual typed text.
- The entry title.
- The entry date.
- Its page number.
- The parent entry identifier.
- The archived or unarchived status.

When the user confirms the paper-transfer animation, archive the corresponding page in the application.

The 3D animation must not be the only place where the writing exists.

If browser-local persistence is implemented, use an appropriate storage mechanism so that entries remain available after a refresh and a later visit in the same browser.

For a more permanent, account-based journaling product, use an appropriate database and authentication layer. Make that integration a separate, clearly identified requirement rather than assuming that Spline itself automatically supplies a complete storage backend.

The real typing interface should support normal text entry, editing, spaces, punctuation, newlines, and long entries. Keep the user's underlying text accurate even where an older typewriter would not physically support every modern character.

## 16. PERFORMANCE AND RESPONSIVENESS

Make the scene feel responsive on ordinary modern laptops and reasonably capable mobile devices.

Prioritize the typewriter and diary over the complexity of the distant environment.

Use reusable key animations, sensible texture resolution, efficient geometry, limited particles, and restrained shadows.

Avoid constructing hundreds of unnecessarily complex mechanical objects when a smaller number of well-modelled, logically grouped parts can create the same visual effect.

On smaller screens, adjust the camera composition and simplify the interface while keeping the text legible.

Allow the user to skip lengthy transitions, reduce motion, and return quickly to the active-writing mode.

The typewriter must respond promptly during normal typing. Do not delay input while a decorative animation is running.

