## 18. FINAL QUALITY REQUIREMENTS

The final experience must prioritize the following, in order:

1. An exceptionally detailed, luxurious, antique mechanical typewriter.
2. Authentic key movements and an unmistakable mechanical typing experience.
3. Real user text appearing accurately on the paper as they type.
4. A physically convincing platen, paper-feed system, and carriage return.
5. Beautiful aged paper with readable, crisp text.
6. A cinematic, believable transition when a completed sheet is transferred into the diary.
7. A thick, richly textured, antique leather-bound diary that preserves the user's writing.
8. A refined library environment and excellent cinematic lighting.
9. An intuitive interface for saving, editing, and browsing memories.
10. Responsive performance and smooth, purposeful motion.

Do not replace the mechanical typewriter with a modern keyboard. Do not replace the diary with a flat image. Do not generate pretend text unrelated to the actual user input. Do not discard a filled page after its animation.

Build this as one coherent interactive experience in which the typewriter is the instrument of creation and the antique diary is the permanent archive.

The emotional ending of every writing session should feel like this:

The user finishes typing. The final character strikes the parchment. The machine returns its carriage with a gentle mechanical flourish. A completed sheet is released, lifted carefully through the warm library light, and placed into the open leather-bound diary. The page settles into its place, joining the user's collection of preserved memories.

The typewriter is ready for the next thought.

The diary keeps the last one.

Make the mechanical details beautiful, the animations deeply satisfying, and the writing experience genuinely functional.